==English==

===Etymology===
Greek: αυθεντικός, from ''authentes'' = author
Both the adjective "authentic" -- a root of the word “authenticity” -- and the noun "author" begin with the same sound: that of the word "awe".  Should we conclude from this that authenticity is a quality of energy faintly reminiscent of what we feel when we describe an experience as awesome?  The fact that the "th" probably comes from the Greek word, "theos", meaning god, supports this hypothesis.  In this context the existence of the habit many people have of conferring the title of "author" on someone who has had his or her writing published is interesting, for conventionally we refer to those whose writings have not been (officially) published as “just writers” -- implying that there are very many writers but that authors are rarer and their work more awesome.  Both these facts support the idea that there is awe in authenticity.
http://www.authentixcoaches.com/readingmaterial2.html.  --[[User:Anguscg|Anguscg]] 20:59, 30 November 2007 (UTC)

===Noun===
'''authenticity'''

# the quality of being authentic or of established authority for truth and correctness.
# Genuineness; the quality of being genuine or not corrupted from the original.
# truthfulness of origins, attributions, commitments, sincerity, and intentions; not a copy or forgery.
